WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.208
MAX SALTONSTALL: Hey, Sara,
if I know some people who

00:00:02.208 --> 00:00:05.060
want to build a machine-learning
pipeline end-to-end on GCP,

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:05.877
could you help?

00:00:05.877 --> 00:00:06.960
SARA ROBINSON: Absolutely.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.293
It's an area I know pretty well.

00:00:08.293 --> 00:00:09.120
How can I help?

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:09.720
MAX SALTONSTALL:
Could you walk me

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.217
through a real-world example?

00:00:11.217 --> 00:00:12.300
SARA ROBINSON: No problem.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.500
We've got a demo
that shows you how

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.270
to predict the price
of a bottle of wine

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:18.200
with nothing more than its
description and variety.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.200
MAX SALTONSTALL: Really?

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.900
You don't even need to know the
name or the year of the wine?

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.910
SARA ROBINSON: Nope, we can
get everything we need just

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:24.900
from those two things.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.820
Give me five minutes, and I'll
show you a wine prediction

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:27.598
model.

00:00:27.598 --> 00:00:29.988
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:35.932
OK, let me explain
how our model works.

00:00:35.932 --> 00:00:38.140
The trick to predicting the
price of a bottle of wine

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.450
without needing to
know the name or year

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.853
is to use a model that allows
us to extract more meaning

00:00:42.853 --> 00:00:44.020
from the wine's description.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.210
We can't feed the raw text
of our wine descriptions

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.190
directly to the
model, so we'll first

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:49.990
need to encode it in
a numerical format

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:51.940
that our model can understand.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.400
MAX SALTONSTALL: So we turn the
words into arrays of numbers,

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.800
so our description
is in a format

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.098
that the ML algorithms
can more easily use?

00:00:59.098 --> 00:00:59.890
SARA ROBINSON: Yes.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:02.090
And there are many
ways to transform text.

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:04.540
If we use multiple types
of transformations at once,

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.550
our model will be able
to identify more complex

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.005
relationships in our data.

00:01:08.005 --> 00:01:09.880
We'll use what's called
a wide and deep model

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.980
to predict each wine's price.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:13.540
For the wide part
of our model, we'll

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.940
represent our text
as a bag of words,

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.280
which will look like an
array of ones and zeros

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:20.500
indicating the presence or
absence of certain words

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:21.970
from our vocabulary.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:25.630
In this example, our vocabulary
includes the top 12,000 words

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:28.730
from our entire text corpus
of wine descriptions.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:31.202
MAX SALTONSTALL: So what
makes this a wide model?

00:01:31.202 --> 00:01:32.410
SARA ROBINSON: Good question.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:34.360
We'll be transforming
each text description

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:38.110
into a single long vector, the
same size as our vocabulary--

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:41.290
in this case, 12,000 elements.

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.240
For the deep part
of our model, we'll

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.858
create word embeddings, which
is just a fancy mathematical way

00:01:45.858 --> 00:01:47.650
of saying that we'll
use vectors to measure

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:49.630
the similarity between words.

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.602
Similar words will be closer
together in vector space.

00:01:52.602 --> 00:01:54.310
MAX SALTONSTALL: Ah,
so we call this deep

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.470
because, instead of using
a single long vector,

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.600
we use many shorter
vectors to capture

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.192
words that are closely related?

00:02:00.192 --> 00:02:01.150
SARA ROBINSON: Exactly.

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:03.067
And the number of vectors
for each description

00:02:03.067 --> 00:02:05.600
depends on how big you want
your embedding space to be.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.600
Here, we're using an
eight-dimensional embedding

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:08.487
space.

00:02:08.487 --> 00:02:10.070
MAX SALTONSTALL: OK,
I think I get it.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:12.222
So how do we actually
train this model?

00:02:12.222 --> 00:02:14.180
MAX SALTONSTALL: Collab
has everything we need.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:16.240
It's an interactive and
collaborative Python notebook

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:18.157
that you can access
directly from the browser,

00:02:18.157 --> 00:02:19.510
completely free.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:22.240
To train our model, we'll
use a TPU, which stands

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.250
for tensor processing unit--

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.350
Google's custom ASIC
designed specifically

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:27.588
for machine learning.

00:02:27.588 --> 00:02:30.130
It's just three mouse clicks to
change your runtime in Collab

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:32.140
to use a TPU.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.840
After we select the TPU
runtime, we'll download the data

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.115
and convert it to a format
our model will understand.

00:02:37.115 --> 00:02:39.490
MAX SALTONSTALL: But what
about all the storage, compute,

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:41.593
and other stuff I need
to do this training?

00:02:41.593 --> 00:02:42.760
MAX SALTONSTALL: Good point.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.843
With Collab, we don't have
to worry about managing

00:02:44.843 --> 00:02:45.947
any infrastructure.

00:02:45.947 --> 00:02:48.280
We just create a notebook and
get straight into building

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.823
and training our model.

00:02:49.823 --> 00:02:51.490
Let's see what the
model looks like when

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:53.865
we use it to predict how much
a particular bottle of wine

00:02:53.865 --> 00:02:55.120
costs.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:56.033
Pretty good, right?

00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:56.950
MAX SALTONSTALL: Yeah.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.992
It looks to be within
about 10% for most of them.

00:02:58.992 --> 00:03:01.117
SARA ROBINSON: You can also
run the model yourself,

00:03:01.117 --> 00:03:02.790
